<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">David VII Ulu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1215">1215</a>–<a href="/years/1270">1270</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as David Ulu · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">David VII, called Ulu ('the Elder'), was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> from 1247 to 1270, reigning at first jointly with his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-vi-narin">David VI Narin</a> under Mongol overlordship. An illegitimate son of George IV, he was recognised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> as co-king, and after David Narin withdrew to western Georgia around 1259 he ruled the eastern kingdom alone, serving in Mongol campaigns and enduring heavy Mongol taxation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-vii-ulu.jpg" alt="David VII Ulu" width="320" height="537" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 13th century artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_David_VII_Ulugh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>David was the son of King George IV of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a> and a woman of non-noble birth, and the Georgian nobility regarded him as illegitimate, so on George's death in 1223 the throne passed to his sister Rusudan. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> became a Mongol vassal in 1236, Rusudan, fearing his claim, sent him into captivity at the court of her son-in-law, Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-ii">Kaykhusraw II</a> of Rum. He was released after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kose-dag">Battle of Köse Dağ</a> in 1243, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> subdued the sultanate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Rusudan died in 1245 while her son David was at the Mongol court, part of the nobility, believing him dead, proclaimed David Ulu king. In 1246 both Davids attended the enthronement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyuk-khan">Güyük Khan</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> divided the nobles into two parties and named David VII the senior ruler, ulu, and his cousin the junior, narin; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju Noyan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> backed David Ulu. Georgian and Armenian troops served in the Mongol conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> in 1256, which David Ulu led in person, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1258, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> lost tens of thousands of soldiers in these campaigns.</p><p>In 1260, when Hulegu Khan called for Georgian troops for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, David Ulu rebelled. A Mongol army under Arghun Aqa defeated him and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargis-i-jaqeli">Sargis I Jaqeli</a> near Akhaldaba and plundered the country; the king fled to Imereti, and his wife Gvantsa was killed. Peace came in 1262, when he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> as Hulegu's vassal. His troops fought for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, defending the Siba fortress in 1263 and driving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke</a>'s forces out of Shirvan in 1265. In 1266 the new ilkhan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa Khan</a>, took Sargis Jaqeli, prince of Samtkhe, under his protection, raising him to David's rank and leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> divided into three.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1270 David led Georgian and Armenian troops for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa</a> against Tekuder, who had taken refuge in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. He died of a bowel infection that spring, aged 55, and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a>. He married four times, to the Mongol Jigda-Khatun, the Alan Altun, Gvantsa of Racha and Esukan, daughter of the Mongol general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chormaqan">Chormaqan</a>, and was succeeded by his son with Gvantsa, Demetrius II.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “David VII” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1240s the Mongols recognised two cousins as co-kings of Georgia: <b>David VII Ulu</b> and David VI Narin, son of Queen Rusudan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.72,44.79&amp;year=1250" aria-label="Tbilisi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5641 677 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of David VII Ulu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6091 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6091 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.72,44.79&amp;year=1250">What was here in 1250? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
